\title{Shell, Make, and CI Style Guide}
\author{Ivan Jacob Agaloos Pesigan}
\date{}

\begin{document}

\maketitle

This guide covers shell scripts, Makefiles, and GitHub workflows.
Agents follow \texttt{.setup/ai/style/shell.md},
which follows this guide and is changed together with it.
All checks in \texttt{make lint} are warnings.
\texttt{make lint} runs shellcheck when the image has it; CI always does.

\section{Shell}

\begin{itemize}
	\item Start with \texttt{\#!/usr/bin/env bash},
	then a comment with what the script does and a usage line.
	\item Set \texttt{set -Eeuo pipefail} before the first command.
	A helper that is only sourced says ``sourced'' in its header and sets nothing.
	A command that may fail on purpose is handled where it runs.
	\item Quote every expansion, and use \texttt{"\$@"}.
	\item Never hard-code a user's home or scratch folder in a template.
	\item Check a path before \texttt{rm -rf}:
	never build it from a variable that can be empty.
	\item Scripts are shellcheck-clean; a disabled check says why.
\end{itemize}

\section{Long Runs}

A run that must outlive the session starts detached,
writes a status file (\texttt{running}, then \texttt{done} or \texttt{STOPPED: <reason>})
and a log next to it,
refuses a second run while the status says \texttt{running},
traps signals so that the status names the reason,
and takes a \texttt{[first]} argument to resume after a failure.

\section{Make}

\begin{itemize}
	\item Targets assume they already run inside the container:
	a recipe never calls \texttt{apptainer}.
	\item Each target is a short call to one R function or script.
	\item Use portable make: R CMD check warns about GNU extensions under \texttt{inst}.
	\item A target that needs SSH has a host script,
	since the container has no keys on Roar.
\end{itemize}

\section{CI}

\begin{itemize}
	\item Use a release tag for an action, never a moving branch,
	and one version of each action across workflows.
	Use a commit SHA only for an action with no release tag.
	\item Jobs that use the container read it from the lock,
	so that a build uses the release's image.
	\item Tokens come only from Actions secrets, never from a file.
	\item \texttt{Template()} writes the workflows:
	change them in rProject's \texttt{inst/template}.
\end{itemize}

